STEP 2 -- THE SERVICE GATE, MEASURED.  🔴 BGP IS **NOT** SERVICE-GATED ON THIS BUILD
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: settle, first-hand and constructively, whether `service bgp` is required before
`router bgp`, so that nothing later in this case can be mistaken for a platform gap.  Group C
measured OSPF and RIP as strictly service-gated (platforms/x960.md 7A.1: `router ospf 1` sent
~1 s after `service ospf` is REFUSED), and the dispatch carried that forward as "service bgp
first, and ALONE".  It does not hold for BGP, and the measurement is unambiguous.

  (a) bgpd is ALREADY RUNNING at baseline, with no `service bgp` in any running-config:
        swi_d# show process | include bgp  ->  1721 bgpd  3  0.0  0.1  20 sleep
        swi_b# show process | include bgp  ->  2281 bgpd  3  0.0  0.5  20 sleep
        swi_b# show running-config | include service
          -> service password-encryption / service ssh / no service telnet / service http /
             no service dhcp-server      (NO bgp line of any kind, and swi_b has never been
             given a `service bgp` command by anyone)
      And unlike OSPF at group C's start, the show commands ANSWER instead of refusing:
        swi_d# show ip bgp summary -> (empty output, no `%` line)
        swi_d# show ip bgp         -> (empty output, no `%` line)
      compare group C: `show ip ospf` -> `% OSPF protocol daemon is not running or feature
      license is not available`.

  (b) `router bgp` with NO `service bgp` -- ACCEPTED IMMEDIATELY (22:51:15, tools/cfg.py):
        awplus# configure terminal
        Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
        awplus(config)# router bgp 65001
        awplus(config-router)#            <- accepted, 0.8 s after entering config mode,
                                             no refusal, no wait, no `service bgp` anywhere
        awplus(config-router)# end
      Read back (22:51:30):
        awplus# show running-config router bgp
        !
        router bgp 65001
         bgp graceful-restart
        !
        awplus# show running-config | include bgp   ->  router bgp 65001 / bgp graceful-restart
                                                        (NO `service bgp` line was added)
        awplus# show process | include bgp          ->  1721 bgpd  (SAME pid -- `router bgp`
                                                        did not start a daemon; it was up)

  (c) What `service bgp` actually is on this build (tools/qmark.py, read-only `?` probe --
      never `<cmd>` + CR, memory `never-send-cli-help-through-a-cr-driver`):
        awplus(config)# service ?
          ...  bgp    Enable BGP service modes  ...
        awplus(config)# service bgp ?
          evpn  Enable EVPN
      and the bare command is incomplete (22:52:18):
        awplus(config)# service bgp
        % Incomplete command.
        awplus(config)# do show running-config | include service
        service ssh / no service telnet / service http / no service rip / no service ospf /
        no service dhcp-server          <- unchanged; nothing was added

  ==> 🔴 FINDING F-6 (new).  **`service bgp` on this x960 build is not the BGPv4 daemon gate.
      Its only completion is `evpn`, and the bare form is `% Incomplete command.`  bgpd runs
      from boot on both an `atlnz`-licensed x960-56 and a Base-licensed x960-28, and
      `router bgp <asn>` is accepted with no preparation at all.**  BGPv4 therefore behaves
      DIFFERENTLY from OSPFv2 and RIP on the same software, which are strictly gated by
      `service ospf` / `service rip` (platforms/x960.md 7A.1).  Two consequences:
        - a tester that sends `service bgp` first, as the carried-forward instruction said,
          gets `% Incomplete command.` -- and with a stop-on-error driver that `%` ABANDONS
          THE REST OF THE BATCH, so the whole BGP configuration silently does not happen.
          That is the 7A.1 trap with the sign reversed, and it would read as "BGP not working".
        - T43858 will NOT leave a `no service bgp` sticky line (F-5), because there is no
          `service bgp` to negate.  The queue's prediction that group D would add a third
          sticky line is WRONG, and the end-of-group reload of swi_d is needed only for the
          two lines group C left.

  STEP 2 VERDICT: PASS -- the gate question is settled by measurement in both directions.
  Nothing in this case may be graded UNSUPPORTED on the strength of a service-gate message,
  because no service gate exists for BGPv4 here.


