  🔴 FINDING F-7, FOUND BY THIS LIMB -- THE SAVED CONFIG AND THE DISPLAYED CONFIG DISAGREE
  ABOUT `bgp graceful-restart`, AND THE WIRE AGREES WITH THE SAVED CONFIG.
    - Creating `router bgp 65001` makes `show running-config` render ` bgp graceful-restart`
      (nobody configured it; it appears by itself).  That is what T43858/swi_d.cfg holds, line
      145, because that file is a capture of `show running-config`.
    - `copy running-config flash:/ckT43858.cfg` at the same moment wrote the OPPOSITE:
        awplus# show file flash:/ckT43858.cfg | include graceful
         no bgp graceful-restart
    - After booting from that file, `show running-config router bgp` reads:
        router bgp 65001
         bgp router-id 1.1.1.1
         no bgp graceful-restart
         neighbor 10.0.41.2 remote-as 65003
         neighbor 10.0.61.2 remote-as 65002
    - 🔴 THE WIRE SETTLES IT.  The DUT's own BGP OPEN, captured in step 6b(1) **while
      running-config was displaying ` bgp graceful-restart`**, advertised these capabilities
      and no others:
        Multiprotocol Extensions (1) AFI IPv4 / SAFI Unicast
        Route Refresh (Cisco) (128)
        Route Refresh (2)
        32-Bit AS Number (65), 4 Byte AS 65001
      **There is no Graceful Restart capability (64) in it.**  And after the reboot:
        awplus# show ip bgp neighbors 10.0.61.2
          BGP state = Established, up for 00:01:53
          Last read 00:01:53, hold time is 90, keepalive interval is 30 seconds
          Neighbor capabilities:
            Route refresh: advertised and received (old and new)
            4-Octet ASN Capability: advertised and received
            Address family IPv4 Unicast: advertised and received
      -- again no graceful restart, in either direction.
    ⇒ So graceful restart was never actually enabled; the **`show running-config` DISPLAY was
      the thing that was wrong**, and the saved file and the protocol both say `no`.  This is
      the same class as `show mirror` (platforms/x960.md 5) and `show ip route database` (7.3):
      a read-back that does not reflect the dataplane.  It is cosmetic for THIS case -- nothing
      in T43858 depends on graceful restart -- but a user who configures `bgp graceful-restart`
      and verifies it with `show running-config` would be misled in the other direction too.
      **FOR THE TEST ENGINEER / a developer: which of the two is intended?**  Recorded, not
      asked (the Test Engineer is away), and it does NOT change this case's verdict.
    Incidental but useful: this also means a `.cfg` deliverable captured from
    `show running-config` is not byte-identical to what `copy running-config` would write.
    T43858/swi_d.cfg is a faithful capture of the DISPLAY, as logged-output.md 2 specifies.

  Measured BGP timers on this build, for the next run:
    hold time 90 s, keepalive interval 30 s (both from `show ip bgp neighbors`, and the 90 s
    hold time is confirmed independently in the OPEN decode of step 6b).

