7. RUN QUALITY
------------------------------------------------------------
  CLEAN.  Nine counted dataplane legs (L1, L2, L3, L4, L5, L6, L7, L8, L9), every one carrying
  its subject(s), an unrouted control that was 0/5 in all nine, and the DUT's own address as an
  instrument control that was 5/5 in all nine.  Four protocol-packet captures on the DUT itself.
  No step was skipped.
  🔴 ONE TESTER ERROR, CORRECTED BEFORE HAND-BACK AND LEFT IN THE RECORD RATHER THAN QUIETLY
  FIXED: step 6a was first written up as "the port mirror copied nothing", a fourth instance of
  the platforms/x960.md section 5 defect class.  **That was false.**  The 0-byte file size came
  from an unverified kill leaving tcpdump running with its buffer unflushed; the same file,
  once the process was really stopped at hand-back, holds the mirrored frames with timestamps
  inside the leg's own window.  The claim is retracted in 6a, in the findings below, in the
  campaign queue and in platforms/x960.md.  No counted leg and no verdict ever depended on it.
  The lesson that replaces it -- a capture file's SIZE read after an unverified kill is not a
  measurement -- is more useful than the finding would have been.
  Two driver incidents, both detected immediately by reading output rather than exit codes,
  both repaired and verified before any further measurement, both in step 6a: the cfg.py batch
  broken by the mirror-port advisory left port1.0.48 shut and out of its VLAN for ~2 minutes,
  and the stray tcpdump ran on eth4 until hand-back, when it was found by `ps` and stopped by
  PID.  No counted leg ran during the port-shut window.
  Nothing was asked of the Test Engineer; nothing in this case needed them.


